.TH mesg "" "Command"
.SH NAME
mesg \- permit/deny messages from other users
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmesg [\fIstate\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
mesg -- permit/deny messages from other users
Usage:	mesg [ state ]
States:
	n	Disallow messages
	y	Allow messages
With no arguments, mesg prints the state.
.HE
.B mesg
controls whether other users may send messages to the caller's
terminal with
.B write
or
.BR msg .
It does so by turning the group/other execute bit of the terminal's
device file (which
.B write
and
.B msg
must be able to open for writing) on or off; the terminal is taken to
be the caller's standard error.
.PP
The
.I state
argument is
.B y
or
.B yes
to allow messages, or
.B n
or
.B no
to disallow them.
With no argument,
.B mesg
prints the current state,
.B yes
or
.BR no ,
and exits with status 0 if messages are allowed, 1 if not.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B mesg
complains and exits with status 2 if the standard error is not a
terminal.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR msg ,
.B write
.SH NOTES
.B mesg
is normally installed set-user-id, so that its execute permission on
the terminal can be changed without giving the caller general write
access to device files.
